INT. DEEP-SEA SURVEY HABITAT "KELP-9" - COMMON ROOM - NIGHT

A cramped steel room with a single round viewport. Beyond it, blackness and drifting silt. Bunks along one wall. A galley table bolted to the floor. A wall speaker with a dented grille.

Three crew members sit at the table playing cards. A fourth voice lives in the speaker.

CAPTAIN HENRIETTA VOSS-LIANG (50s, gray braid, reading glasses on a cord) deals. DR. TAMSIN ODUYA (30s, tall, a bandaged hand) arranges her cards slowly. ENGINEER PIOTR NAKAMURA-BELL (40s, heavy, oil under his nails) eats from a foil pouch with a plastic spoon.

VOSS-LIANG
Your bid, Doctor.

TAMSIN
Pass.

PIOTR
She always passes.

TAMSIN
I always win.

PIOTR
When?

TAMSIN
Eventually.

A low, slow GROAN passes through the hull. Nobody looks up.

VOSS-LIANG
That's the third time today.

PIOTR
It's the sediment settling. Pressure shifts. It's nothing.

TAMSIN
Nothing makes a very specific sound.

The speaker crackles.

TOPSIDE (V.O.)
(over speaker)
Kelp-9, Topside. Weather's picking up. We'll hold the surface buoy another twelve hours, then we're sending the lift. Over.

VOSS-LIANG
(toward speaker)
Copy, Topside. All quiet down here. Out.

She sets her cards face-down.

VOSS-LIANG (CONT'D)
That's the longest any of us has gone without an alarm.

PIOTR
Don't say that.

VOSS-LIANG
Why not?

PIOTR
Because the habitat listens.

TAMSIN
It does not listen. It's a tin can with a sandwich in it.

The GROAN comes again, closer. A pipe overhead ticks. The silt outside the viewport swirls, as if something huge has moved behind it.

Tamsin stands slowly. Walks to the viewport. Presses her bandaged hand to the glass.

TAMSIN (CONT'D)
Captain.

VOSS-LIANG
Doctor?

TAMSIN
The silt isn't settling.

Piotr drops his spoon. He goes to the viewport. Voss-Liang is already there. All three stare out at a patch of black that is slightly blacker than the rest, and that is rising.

TOPSIDE (V.O.)
(over speaker, distorted)
Kelp-9, Topside, we're showing a seismic spike. Repeat, seismic spike. Get out of there. Get out of there now.

The lights flicker. The hull begins to SHRIEK.
